    },
    {
      "title": "Resolution of the Ancient Planet Abundance Question",
      "description": "Historical debate between thinkers like Aristotle and Democritus concerned whether planets were common or Earth was unique. While early 20th-century theories hypothesized that planets formed via rare stellar near-misses (suggesting only one in a trillion stars would have planets), modern observations and simulations reveal that planets are everywhere and every star in the night sky has them."
    }
  ],
